<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">March of the Volunteers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1934">1934</a>, music <a href="/years/1935">1935</a>; first heard publicly May <a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 义勇军进行曲, Yìyǒngjūn Jìnxíngqǔ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;March of the Volunteers&quot; is the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, and unlike earlier Chinese state anthems it is written wholly in vernacular rather than Classical Chinese. It came out of the wave of nationalist art and writing that followed the Japanese invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>March of the Volunteers</b>&quot; is the national anthem of the People's Republic of China, and unlike earlier Chinese state anthems it is written wholly in vernacular rather than Classical Chinese.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Cultural Revolution Tian Han, the author of the official anthem &quot;<b>March of the Volunteers</b>&quot;, was purged, and &quot;The East Is Red&quot; served as China's unofficial anthem; by 1966 Mao had succeeded in having it sung in place of the anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li><li><blockquote>In this the anthem differs from the People's Republic of China's &quot;<b>March of the Volunteers</b>&quot;, written a few years later entirely in the modern vernacular.</blockquote><a href="/songs/three-principles-of-the-people">National Anthem of the Republic of China</a></li></ul></section>
